{
 "slug": "factoring-polynomials",
 "title": "Factoring Polynomials: GCF, Trinomials and Special Patterns",
 "seo_title": "Algebra 1 Factoring Worksheets: GCF, Trinomials, Difference of Squares and Perfect Squares (Free, Answer Key)",
 "short_title": "Factoring polynomials",
 "domain": "Polynomials & Factoring",
 "standard_code": "A-SSE.A.2",
 "standard_url": "https://www.thecorestandards.org/Math/Content/HSA/SSE/A/2/",
 "standard_text": "Use the structure of an expression to identify ways to rewrite it. For example, see x⁴ − y⁴ as (x²)² − (y²)², thus recognizing it as a difference of squares that can be factored as (x² − y²)(x² + y²).",
 "meta_desc": "Free Algebra 1 factoring worksheets (A-SSE.A.2): greatest common factor, factoring x² + bx + c and ax² + bx + c, difference of squares, perfect square trinomials and factoring completely. 100 questions with an answer key.",
 "key_big": "Take out the GCF first. Then find two numbers that multiply to c and add to b.",
 "key_sub": "A² − B² = (A − B)(A + B). Check every answer by multiplying back.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p><b>Always take out the greatest common factor (GCF) first.</b> Then look at what is left:</p><ul><li><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>bx</i> + <i>c</i>: find two numbers that <b>multiply to <i>c</i></b> and <b>add to <i>b</i></b>.</li><li><i>ax</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>bx</i> + <i>c</i>: find two numbers that multiply to <i>ac</i> and add to <i>b</i>, split the middle term and group.</li><li><i>A</i><sup>2</sup> − <i>B</i><sup>2</sup> = (<i>A</i> − <i>B</i>)(<i>A</i> + <i>B</i>); <i>A</i><sup>2</sup> ± 2<i>AB</i> + <i>B</i><sup>2</sup> = (<i>A</i> ± <i>B</i>)<sup>2</sup>.</li></ul><p>Check by multiplying back. A sum of squares like <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 9 cannot be factored.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Factor <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 3<i>x</i> − 18.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Multiply to −18, add to −3: −6 and 3.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: (<i>x</i> − 6)(<i>x</i> + 3)</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Factor 3<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 10<i>x</i> + 8.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>ac</i> = 24: 6 and 4.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 6<i>x</i> + 4<i>x</i> + 8 = 3<i>x</i>(<i>x</i> + 2) + 4(<i>x</i> + 2).</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: (3<i>x</i> + 4)(<i>x</i> + 2)</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Factor completely 2<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 50.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>GCF 2: 2(<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 25).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Difference of squares.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 2(<i>x</i> − 5)(<i>x</i> + 5)</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Taking out a common factor that is not the greatest: 12<i>x</i> + 18 = 6(2<i>x</i> + 3), not 2(6<i>x</i> + 9).</li><li>Choosing numbers that multiply to <i>c</i> but do not add to <i>b</i>.</li><li>Sign errors when <i>c</i> is negative: one number must be positive and one negative.</li><li>Stopping too early: 2<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 50 = 2(<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 25) still factors.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "How do you factor x² + bx + c?",
   "a": "Find two numbers that multiply to c and add to b. If they are p and q, the factored form is (x + p)(x + q)."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you factor when the leading coefficient is not 1?",
   "a": "Use the AC method: multiply a and c, find two numbers that multiply to ac and add to b, split the middle term and factor by grouping."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is a difference of squares?",
   "a": "An expression like A² − B². It factors as (A − B)(A + B), for example x² − 49 = (x − 7)(x + 7)."
  },
  {
   "q": "What does factor completely mean?",
   "a": "Keep factoring until no factor can be broken down further, always starting with the greatest common factor."
  }
 ],
